I want you to write a non-technical sketch of 
- what the problem is (matching themes) and 
- why jaccard or Hungarian matching is unhelpful. 
- Also why cosine similarities hard to interpret, even if we do things like apply exponential decay (this helps, but still leaves the scale/metric undetermined. is .3 a big change or small? is .7 similar or not?) maybe talk about how cosine sim is good for ranking and work to validate embedding models focusses on ranking/ordering rather than trying to quantify similarity 

- explain why OT is a nice idea (allows themes to split) but not optimal because mass must be matched, even when theme doesn't exist
- explain UOT and why it's preferable, but highlight that the loss/penalty parameter is arbitrary and not necessarily aligned with human judgements - e.g of whether two themes are sufficiently similar to be considered together

- talk about how we'd like metrics for how similar/different sets of themes are (e.g. to judge differences between populations, or stability of multiple runs of an analysis)

- then, talk about how we could set K. 
- One option might be to set it so that themes never split into > 3 (this seems like a reasonable heuristic, but still subjective). 
- but another way is to try and calibrate K so it corresponds to typical human judgements
- this introduces the idea of an experiment based on human similarity ratings which can be used to calibrate K

idea 1 is to use STS ratings and calibrate K so that the mid-point of that scale corresponds to a K that allows themes to split if they have a similar cosine distance

idea 2 is to delibeately collect new human judgements about themes and explicitly askl whethe the themes should be split or lumped etc (as we discused above)

The sketch should give details of the two kinds of expeirments and how they could be run to calibrate K, but in general terms. Later we can add a methods section.


my feeling is that at the moment I may only have time to use idea 1 with exisitng data, so focus on that and check the existing datasets and think carfull about how they could be used to address the problem of how to calibrate K


